# logsentry.conf — LogSentry configuration
#
# Format: KEY=VALUE, one per line. Values may be quoted. Lines starting with #
# are comments. This file is PARSED, not sourced — shell metacharacters have no
# special meaning and unknown keys are a hard error, so a typo fails at
# start-up instead of silently monitoring nothing.
#
# Precedence:  command-line flags  >  this file  >  built-in defaults
#
# Install to /etc/logsentry/logsentry.conf and `chmod 600` it. A world-writable
# config lets any local user redirect what the (root) monitor watches.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What to monitor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated list of files, directories (walked recursively) and globs.
# The defaults below cover the logs an attacker touches first: authentication,
# sudo, and the web server access log.
#
# Debian / Ubuntu:
WATCH_PATHS="/var/log/auth.log /var/log/syslog /var/log/sudo.log /var/log/nginx/*.log"
#
# RHEL / CentOS / Fedora — uncomment instead of the line above:
# WATCH_PATHS="/var/log/secure /var/log/messages /var/log/audit/audit.log /var/log/httpd/*_log"
#
# macOS (for local testing):
# WATCH_PATHS="/var/log/system.log /var/log/install.log"

# Basenames or paths to skip. Compressed archives are excluded by default:
# they are logrotate's output, they never change again once written, and
# hashing them on every pass is wasted I/O.
EXCLUDE_PATTERNS="*.gz *.bz2 *.xz *.zip *.tar"

# Do not descend into symlinked files or directories. Leave this at 0 unless
# you have a specific reason: a symlink swap is itself a common way to redirect
# a monitor away from the file it thinks it is watching.
FOLLOW_SYMLINKS=0

# Skip files larger than this many bytes (0 = no limit). Useful on hosts with
# multi-gigabyte access logs where a full re-hash every minute is too costly;
# consider raising CHECK_INTERVAL instead of skipping the file entirely.
MAX_FILE_SIZE=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the baseline, seal, event log and scan history live. On a production
# host, put this on separate storage from the logs themselves — ideally
# read-only or append-only media — so an attacker who owns /var/log does not
# automatically own the evidence that /var/log changed.
STATE_DIR=/var/lib/logsentry

# md5 (fast, and what this project is specified around) or sha256.
#
# MD5 is broken for *collision resistance*: an adversary can construct two
# different files with the same digest. It is not broken for the property this
# tool relies on, which is second-preimage resistance — given a log file that
# already exists, producing different content with the same MD5 is not
# practical. MD5 is the right default here because it is roughly twice as fast
# on large files and universally available. Switch to sha256 when a compliance
# regime demands it (PCI DSS 11.5, FIPS environments).
HASH_ALGO=md5

# Also write a classic `<file>.md5` sidecar next to every monitored file, in
# the format `md5sum -c` accepts. Interoperable with the manual workflow, but
# it writes into the log directory, which some hardened hosts audit.
SIDECAR_MD5=0

# Seal the baseline with HMAC-SHA256 instead of a plain digest. This is what
# stops an attacker from editing the logs, rebuilding the baseline, and
# re-sealing it to look clean.
#
# The key is only meaningful if it is NOT reachable from the host being
# monitored — keep it on your management station and pass it in at check time:
#
#     BASELINE_SEAL_KEY=$(cat /mnt/usb/logsentry.key) logsentry check
#
# BASELINE_SEAL_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Alerting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum severity that triggers an alert: INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, CRITICAL.
#
# LOW is the right production default. Dropping to INFO alerts on every normal
# append and every logrotate run, which trains people to ignore the channel.
ALERT_MIN_SEVERITY=LOW

# Human-readable findings on stderr.
ALERT_CONSOLE=1

# Append one JSON object per finding, ECS-shaped, ready for Filebeat/Fluent Bit
# to pick up. Point your log shipper at this file.
ALERT_FILE=/var/log/logsentry/events.jsonl

# Send findings to syslog via logger(1) with facility auth. Severity maps to
# crit / err / warning / notice, so existing SIEM rules on auth.crit fire
# without any new parsing.
ALERT_SYSLOG=1
ALERT_SYSLOG_TAG=logsentry

# Slack or Microsoft Teams incoming webhook. One digest message per scan, not
# one per file. Leave empty to disable.
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/XXX/YYY/ZZZ

# Email digest via mail(1). Requires a working local MTA. Leave empty to
# disable.
# ALERT_EMAIL=soc@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Watch mode
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seconds between passes for `logsentry watch`.
#
# This is a polling monitor, and the interval is the detection window: an
# attacker who edits a log and restores its size within the interval is still
# caught (content is hashed, not just size), but one who edits and then removes
# the tool has a window of this length. 60s is a reasonable balance; drop to
# 10-15s on high-value hosts, and see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md for why real-time
# inotify-based watching is deliberately not implemented.
CHECK_INTERVAL=60
